00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:52.120
to change everything in telco.

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:55.240
All right, so what
is charging, right.

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:59.260
Everyone here, I see
everyone has a phone,

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:01.910
and all your phones
have plans, right.

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:04.870
The telcos come up
with designs to get

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:08.620
you to become a subscriber,
that's like a customer to them,

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:11.810
number of minutes, number of
texts, the amount of data.

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:13.890
And so there's
marketing teams that

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:16.660
design those plans to entice
you to become a customer

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:17.709
and stay with them.

00:04:17.709 --> 00:04:21.490
And then as you're making phone
calls, as you're sending text,

00:04:21.490 --> 00:04:24.520
there is software
somewhere, usually sitting

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:26.740
on their network,
usually sitting

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:32.080
on the ground on premise,
that's tracking your calls

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:34.110
and deducting them
from your plan.

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:37.840
And so the United States is
largely a postpaid market,

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:42.880
meaning we pay our bills
after the month is over,

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:47.830
but the world is largely
a prepaid market, right.

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:49.960
Most people are paying
for it ahead of time,

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:52.660
and then when they're out
of minutes or out of data,

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:54.370
it stops you, right.

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:59.380
So this, it needs to
be correct, right.

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:01.840
It's a very technically
difficult problem

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:03.090
to solve, right.

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:07.300
It's revenue, right, so if
the calculation is ever wrong,

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:11.290
these guys are not being able
to calculate revenue, which

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:14.240
is obviously their business.

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:16.570
Number two, it's super
high volume, right.

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:19.900
Think of all the
people in the country

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:25.240
simultaneously making calls and
doing texts, so lots of volume

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:26.010
going on.

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:31.589
And it has to be low latency,
right, so when you make a call,

00:05:31.589 --> 00:05:33.880
you're not going to sit there
and wait for the charging

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:36.010
engine to return in a minute.

00:05:36.010 --> 00:05:38.350
It has to be
milliseconds of time.

00:05:38.350 --> 00:05:42.420
So it's a super difficult,
technical problem to solve,

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:46.900
and I call it the
trifecta of hard, right.

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:51.270
Transactional integrity, has
to be computing correctly.

00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:55.760
Low latency, and this is
high growth, high volume.

00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:58.700
Now, all of us
are-- or most of us,

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:01.850
maybe I have a
couple of Canadians.

00:06:01.850 --> 00:06:03.380
I know I have a
couple of Canadians.

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:06.140
But you know, we kind
of think of the US

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:09.440
as like this really big
market, and the world

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:11.060
revolves around us.

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:14.450
But the reality is,
we are tiny compared

00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:18.120
to some of these other markets
like India and China, right.

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:21.500
And so we have a
customer located in India

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:24.360
that has 200
million subscribers.

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:28.640
And so imagine all of
us in the United States,

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:29.942
we just had one telco.

00:06:29.942 --> 00:06:31.400
We were all there,
and there wasn't

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:35.930
choices like Verizon and AT&amp;T
and Sprint and T-Mobile, right.

00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:37.670
We have a customer
that is basically

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:44.600
the size of the United
States, and so for them, this

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:47.100
is a really hard
problem for us to solve.

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:49.910
When we install our
products like I said,

00:06:49.910 --> 00:06:54.510
traditionally on the ground,
we use Oracle, right.

00:06:54.510 --> 00:06:58.720
Oracle's known as maybe
the world's best enterprise

00:06:58.720 --> 00:06:59.680
database.

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:04.120
Its super trusted, it works,
it handles a lot of volume,

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:07.600
and for us, we can
process in our engine

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:11.180
about 50,000 transactions
per second, which is a lot.

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:14.650
And that's good, but
for our India customer,

00:07:14.650 --> 00:07:16.480
for example, it's not enough.

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:19.420
And so our solution
there is to stand up

00:07:19.420 --> 00:07:25.570
copies of the implementation
around the country in India

00:07:25.570 --> 00:07:27.130
to handle urban circles.

00:07:27.130 --> 00:07:31.210
So think of a city or a city
area as an urban circle.

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:35.290
I have 12
implementations, and so

00:07:35.290 --> 00:07:38.290
its kind of like 12
customers in one.

00:07:38.290 --> 00:07:42.730
Like I have a Delhi, I have
a Mumbai, right, Calcutta.

00:07:42.730 --> 00:07:45.040
I have all these
different areas where

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:47.840
they are handling the traffic.

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:52.100
But I need to have a consistent
database because people move,

00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:52.600
right.

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:54.610
You don't just stay in one spot.

00:07:54.610 --> 00:07:59.020
So these guys, the database
has to talk to each other,

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:00.100
I got to keep it up.

00:08:00.100 --> 00:08:03.730
And so today, we have
essentially 12 data centers

00:08:03.730 --> 00:08:08.910
with testing, staging,
production, disaster recovery,

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:09.410
right.

00:08:09.410 --> 00:08:12.010
There's a really good
story about our customer

00:08:12.010 --> 00:08:16.190
where their data center
started to flood in India,

00:08:16.190 --> 00:08:19.450
and our team got
caught in the building.

00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:21.580
And they were just
like lifting up

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:24.070
the machines off of the ground.

00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:27.880
I guess that's one
way of getting cloud.

00:08:27.880 --> 00:08:29.377
But they just
started like lifting

00:08:29.377 --> 00:08:30.460
the machines to save them.

00:08:30.460 --> 00:08:33.697
And our team actually,
I just saw this video,

00:08:33.697 --> 00:08:35.530
our team actually got
caught in the building

00:08:35.530 --> 00:08:37.990
for like three days, they
slept there, no food,

00:08:37.990 --> 00:08:41.030
like managing the machines.

00:08:41.030 --> 00:08:46.850
All right, so like I said,
50,000 transactions per second

00:08:46.850 --> 00:08:49.250
is pretty much our limit.

00:08:49.250 --> 00:08:52.850
And you know, being a
tough, hard problem,

00:08:52.850 --> 00:08:55.520
Oracle really owns
the telco space.

00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:58.250
Like, they're everywhere,
and some of our customers

00:08:58.250 --> 00:09:03.290
pay between $10 million and
$100 million a year to Oracle.

00:09:03.290 --> 00:09:07.280
Oracle is notoriously difficult
to do business with, right.

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:11.350
My customers have told me
that they try to get off,

00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:14.060
and Oracle just increases
the price, right.

00:09:14.060 --> 00:09:15.515
So if you're paying
$100 million,

00:09:15.515 --> 00:09:17.640
and you figured out a way
to carve out $20 million,

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:20.630
they figure out a way to
still charge you $100 million.

00:09:20.630 --> 00:09:24.140
And so our customers were
like, you know, what can we do.

00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:26.750
Now, there's a lot of
database vendors out there

00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:29.390
and we certainly looked
at a lot of them,

00:09:29.390 --> 00:09:34.130
but we were like, you know,
to really change this problem

00:09:34.130 --> 00:09:40.310
and come up with a fresh idea,
we went to the engineering team

00:09:40.310 --> 00:09:45.080
and said, could we make our
product 10 times faster, right.

00:09:45.080 --> 00:09:48.170
Assuming that there's
nothing stopping you,

00:09:48.170 --> 00:09:51.050
right, change out Oracle,
change out the coding,

00:09:51.050 --> 00:09:53.650
rewrite the whole entire engine.

00:09:53.650 --> 00:09:54.650
What could we do, right.

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:56.020
Sky's the limit.

00:09:56.020 --> 00:09:59.530
And we started to look
at the database, right.

00:09:59.530 --> 00:10:02.510
This is a write
intensive application.

00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:07.940
It's not just, you
know, look it up,

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:10.960
return an answer to the user.

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:15.110
You actually have to deduct
five minutes from the plan

00:10:15.110 --> 00:10:16.610
and write it to the database.

00:10:16.610 --> 00:10:20.619
Meanwhile, if you're like me,
I'm like texting on my phone

00:10:20.619 --> 00:10:22.160
and then doing
something on my watch,

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:23.930
and you know, I'm
on my iPad, right.

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:28.550
So atomically correct actions
need to be captured and written

00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:29.720
to the database.

00:10:29.720 --> 00:10:33.080
And so we noticed that
a lot of the performance

00:10:33.080 --> 00:10:36.080
of our application was spent
writing to the database.

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:39.020
So that's kind of got
us like on the trail of,

00:10:39.020 --> 00:10:41.780
maybe we could do something
with a different database.

00:10:41.780 --> 00:10:43.750
We looked at a bunch
of different databases,

00:10:43.750 --> 00:10:48.020
and basically we're
like, what can we do.

00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:54.900
Around this time, so
I started in February.

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:59.810
I think Spanner was
announced in March.

00:10:59.810 --> 00:11:01.220
Valentine's Day, OK.

00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:03.290
That was the day
I started Optiva.

00:11:03.290 --> 00:11:04.560
I didn't realize that.

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:06.200
So that was the day
I started Optiva.

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:07.805
And so we were
looking, we were like,

00:11:07.805 --> 00:11:10.550
what can we do, how can we
make this 10 times faster.

00:11:10.550 --> 00:11:14.300
And we read the Spanner press
release, and we're like,

00:11:14.300 --> 00:11:15.980
are you kidding me.

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:18.380
Like, this is our
dream come true, right.

00:11:18.380 --> 00:11:20.900
We have a perfect
database that can handle

00:11:20.900 --> 00:11:22.400
the transactional volume.

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:26.540
It's distributed,
it's consistent,

00:11:26.540 --> 00:11:29.990
multiple writings,
like synchronized

00:11:29.990 --> 00:11:32.630
writing across 1,000 servers.

00:11:32.630 --> 00:11:36.500
It's exactly what we needed
to replace big bad Oracle.

00:11:36.500 --> 00:11:40.070
And it had the Google name,
so that our customers woulf

00:11:40.070 --> 00:11:41.060
trust it, right.

00:11:41.060 --> 00:11:43.190
I mean, they're not
going to just switch to--

00:11:43.190 --> 00:11:45.890
not to disparage any
databases out there,

00:11:45.890 --> 00:11:49.970
but you know, NuoDB or
MongoDB or whatever.

00:11:49.970 --> 00:11:53.180
My customers are like, these are
$10 billion dollar companies.

00:11:53.180 --> 00:11:54.920
They're like, we need
a good strong name.

00:11:54.920 --> 00:11:59.000
So we looked at Spanner and
we're like, this could work.

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:00.690
This might work.

00:12:00.690 --> 00:12:04.220
So I called up my
friends at Google.

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:06.117
Dom, Dom's here.

00:12:06.117 --> 00:12:07.950
I called, I literally
like sent him an email

00:12:07.950 --> 00:12:09.991
and was like, hey, Dom,
you haven't heard from me

00:12:09.991 --> 00:12:12.140
in, I don't know, 19 years.

00:12:12.140 --> 00:12:12.710
Yeah.

00:12:12.710 --> 00:12:15.770
But I just read this really
awesome press release,

00:12:15.770 --> 00:12:17.090
and we think it could work.

00:12:17.090 --> 00:12:18.420
And it was really new.

00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:20.960
And so we put
together two teams,

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:24.710
and we just started working on
the problem, just go, right,

00:12:24.710 --> 00:12:26.390
can we make it 10 times faster.

00:12:26.390 --> 00:12:29.840
And it was pretty easy to make
it 10 times faster, right.

00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:32.210
I'll talk about it in a
second, about like what we did,

00:12:32.210 --> 00:12:36.290
but you know, the fact
is, it was such a game

00:12:36.290 --> 00:12:40.760
changer for telco that we would
have put our entire engineering

00:12:40.760 --> 00:12:45.380
budget behind getting it to work
with Spanner if it was required

00:12:45.380 --> 00:12:50.760
because it was such a
revolutionary database.

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:52.710
So that was really cool.

00:12:52.710 --> 00:12:54.690
So let's see.

00:12:54.690 --> 00:12:55.280
Yeah.

00:12:55.280 --> 00:12:56.690
So I have a demo here.

00:12:56.690 --> 00:12:59.135
I'm going to attempt to click.

00:13:06.660 --> 00:13:07.860
All right.

00:13:07.860 --> 00:13:11.960
When can you go ahead and
switch it to the demo?

00:13:11.960 --> 00:13:15.350
So try to make
performance like cool,

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:18.890
and it's actually really hard
for performance to be cool.

00:13:18.890 --> 00:13:22.040
So the other thing
we wanted to do

00:13:22.040 --> 00:13:24.770
besides making it 10 times
faster, we wanted to make sure

00:13:24.770 --> 00:13:26.160
it was a tenth of the cost.

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:29.450
It needed to be also
cheaper than Oracle, right.

00:13:29.450 --> 00:13:32.980
So again, this system
works for the telcos.

00:13:32.980 --> 00:13:34.460
It's running their revenue.

00:13:34.460 --> 00:13:36.470
They're not going to
move off of a system

00:13:36.470 --> 00:13:38.090
if it's just two times faster.

00:13:38.090 --> 00:13:39.140
It's just not enough.

00:13:39.140 --> 00:13:41.100
Or four times faster.

00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:42.930
Now, it had to be
10 times faster,

00:13:42.930 --> 00:13:45.080
so it had to be big enough
for a reason to change,

00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:47.689
but the same time, if it's
10 times as expensive,

00:13:47.689 --> 00:13:48.980
they're also not going to move.

00:13:48.980 --> 00:13:50.870
So our second goal that
we started working on

00:13:50.870 --> 00:13:54.340
was, could we make it
a tenth of the cost.

00:13:54.340 --> 00:13:56.090
And that's the beauty
of the cloud, right.

00:13:56.090 --> 00:13:57.298
That's the beauty of Spanner.

00:13:57.298 --> 00:14:01.280
So what we have here is,
this is a live demo, right,

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:02.630
so it's not a video.

00:14:02.630 --> 00:14:03.710
It's a real demo.

00:14:03.710 --> 00:14:05.450
What you see over
here are transactions.

00:14:05.450 --> 00:14:08.480
These are call records of users.

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:10.940
You can see kind
of what's going on,

00:14:10.940 --> 00:14:14.450
right, what they're
using, what the story is.

00:14:14.450 --> 00:14:18.410
Over here is the transactions
per second, and I bumped it up.

00:14:18.410 --> 00:14:21.120
I actually need to bump
it up a little bit more.

00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:22.590
It's at 300.

00:14:22.590 --> 00:14:24.680
So this is live.

00:14:24.680 --> 00:14:29.270
I'm going to just
take it up to 500,000

00:14:29.270 --> 00:14:32.160
transactions per second.

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:34.490
And so the engine,
what Spanner is doing

00:14:34.490 --> 00:14:38.000
is dynamically deploying
nodes in Google Cloud

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:42.020
live right now to
build up to this level.

00:14:42.020 --> 00:14:44.780
So imagine what happens
in a country when

00:14:44.780 --> 00:14:48.860
like an earthquake happens, or
you know, it's Christmas Day

00:14:48.860 --> 00:14:50.720
and everyone's making
their family calls.

00:14:50.720 --> 00:14:54.740
You would get this temporary
spike in usage, you know,

00:14:54.740 --> 00:14:55.700
or World Cup, right.

00:14:55.700 --> 00:14:56.991
We just went through World Cup.

00:14:56.991 --> 00:14:59.990
It was like a month long,
lots more usage of video

00:14:59.990 --> 00:15:00.960
on the phone.

00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:05.150
But then after World
Cup, the volume drops.

00:15:05.150 --> 00:15:08.300
What my customers do
is, they provision

00:15:08.300 --> 00:15:12.980
to like just above what they
think they need on their worst

00:15:12.980 --> 00:15:17.600
day, because they get the media
attention when they're down,

00:15:17.600 --> 00:15:19.100
when things aren't working.

00:15:19.100 --> 00:15:21.980
So they need to have data
centers and the capacity

00:15:21.980 --> 00:15:26.750
to compute already hot and
live, plus the disaster

00:15:26.750 --> 00:15:30.355
recovery for all of that, to
be able to handle their best

00:15:30.355 --> 00:15:31.730
day or their worst
day, depending

00:15:31.730 --> 00:15:33.440
on how you look at it, right.

00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:36.560
What's so great about
Spanner is that it's

00:15:36.560 --> 00:15:38.370
deploying dynamically.

00:15:38.370 --> 00:15:40.700
So maybe there was
an unexpected event,

00:15:40.700 --> 00:15:42.650
you know, a negative
event in the country,

00:15:42.650 --> 00:15:46.490
a terrorist attack,
right, an earthquake.

00:15:46.490 --> 00:15:49.820
You get these spikes, and by
running in the public cloud

00:15:49.820 --> 00:15:51.680
and specifically
running with Spanner,

00:15:51.680 --> 00:15:57.020
I now no longer need to
deploy Oracle instances.

00:15:57.020 --> 00:16:02.240
Now, over here is the big
bad line of Oracle spend.

00:16:02.240 --> 00:16:06.410
Every time that's spiking, I'm
deploying another data center

00:16:06.410 --> 00:16:09.450
that's provisioned for
usually equal capacity.

00:16:09.450 --> 00:16:11.007
So if you're the
customer in India,

00:16:11.007 --> 00:16:12.590
right, you have a
system running about

00:16:12.590 --> 00:16:16.650
and handling about 50,000
transactions per second.

00:16:16.650 --> 00:16:19.760
And now you need to
handle more volume,

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:21.200
usually what they
do is they just

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:24.350
set up another data center
that handles that capacity.

00:16:24.350 --> 00:16:26.000
You don't need
all that capacity,

00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:29.150
but you pre-purchase all the
compute and all the power

00:16:29.150 --> 00:16:30.650
to be able to handle that.

00:16:30.650 --> 00:16:32.720
What's nice about Spanner
is, I can go back down

00:16:32.720 --> 00:16:35.250
and all that spend
comes back down.

00:16:35.250 --> 00:16:36.770
So a lot of times,
when people look

00:16:36.770 --> 00:16:40.670
at public cloud, for
our industry, for telco,

00:16:40.670 --> 00:16:43.160
we're really kind
of behind everyone.

00:16:43.160 --> 00:16:47.990
Everyone's still running
in closets on sites, right.

00:16:47.990 --> 00:16:50.420
Maybe some have virtualized.

00:16:50.420 --> 00:16:52.640
No one's in public cloud.

00:16:52.640 --> 00:16:55.970
In fact, when I
started, my team was

00:16:55.970 --> 00:16:58.130
like don't talk about Google.

00:16:58.130 --> 00:16:59.480
Don't talk about public cloud.

00:16:59.480 --> 00:17:00.620
It's like really bad.

00:17:00.620 --> 00:17:01.980
They're never going to do it.

00:17:01.980 --> 00:17:04.010
And I'm like, this is insane.

00:17:04.010 --> 00:17:06.030
Like, public cloud
is everywhere.

00:17:06.030 --> 00:17:07.940
It's absolutely coming to telco.

00:17:07.940 --> 00:17:12.544
And so it's like kind
of behind, but now we're

00:17:12.544 --> 00:17:14.210
bringing them to the
future here, right.

00:17:14.210 --> 00:17:15.300
The cloud is now.

00:17:15.300 --> 00:17:18.589
So they over-provision,
they pay for all this stuff.

00:17:18.589 --> 00:17:21.050
With Spanner, I don't
have to do that.

00:17:21.050 --> 00:17:24.440
And to show you that it's
live, if I Alt Tab over here,

00:17:24.440 --> 00:17:28.860
this is the Google
product, right,

00:17:28.860 --> 00:17:30.680
showing the transactions.

00:17:30.680 --> 00:17:34.786
You can see at 11 whatever--
why does it say 11:45?

00:17:34.786 --> 00:17:35.285
Maybe 12:45.

00:17:35.285 --> 00:17:36.970
Maybe it's off on the time.

00:17:36.970 --> 00:17:39.392
It spiked when I moved it up.

00:17:39.392 --> 00:17:43.267
The nodes-- I forget why
the nodes are consistent.

00:17:43.267 --> 00:17:44.600
Maybe [INAUDIBLE] can remind me.

00:17:44.600 --> 00:17:46.390
But you can see the transaction.

00:17:46.390 --> 00:17:50.020
So this is live running
in Google Cloud.

00:17:50.020 --> 00:17:52.180
Someone's paying
for it, probably me.

00:17:52.180 --> 00:17:54.920
I'll probably take down the
transactions after this, right,

00:17:54.920 --> 00:17:56.210
because it's kind of too much.

00:17:56.210 --> 00:17:57.860
Remind me to take it down.

00:18:00.590 --> 00:18:05.850
So this is a game changer
for telco and for us.

00:18:05.850 --> 00:18:06.350
So hold on.

00:18:06.350 --> 00:18:08.420
Let me look at my
notes one more time.

00:18:12.320 --> 00:18:13.010
Let's see here.

00:18:15.590 --> 00:18:21.390
I talked without
looking at my notes.

00:18:21.390 --> 00:18:24.960
All right, so why does this--

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:27.460
actually go back to the screen.

00:18:30.170 --> 00:18:31.670
Awesome.

00:18:31.670 --> 00:18:33.840
So why does this work, right.

00:18:33.840 --> 00:18:36.950
So sure, Oracle can
do clusters and can

00:18:36.950 --> 00:18:42.830
kind of replicate multi-writing,
but the difference with Spanner

00:18:42.830 --> 00:18:47.810
is, I need to be distributed
across like thousands

00:18:47.810 --> 00:18:51.650
of servers, right, and I
need that strong consistency.

00:18:51.650 --> 00:18:53.240
So Oracle can't do that, right.

00:18:53.240 --> 00:18:55.880
I mean, I can create a cluster
of like maybe 50 Oracle

00:18:55.880 --> 00:18:57.650
databases, but
Spanner's doing it

00:18:57.650 --> 00:19:00.350
all for me behind the scenes.

00:19:00.350 --> 00:19:02.270
I have to have
global scale, right.

00:19:02.270 --> 00:19:05.000
My telcos have
multiple data centers,

00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:06.950
like my India example has 12.

00:19:06.950 --> 00:19:10.410
But Google, I mean, I
have the whole Google

00:19:10.410 --> 00:19:14.320
footprint behind us, billions
of dollars of investment.

00:19:14.320 --> 00:19:17.130
And I need to have
performance at scale.

00:19:17.130 --> 00:19:19.860
I can't have a choke
point for my writes

00:19:19.860 --> 00:19:21.600
like I do with Oracle.

00:19:21.600 --> 00:19:25.880
And so because of Spanner's
true time functionality,

00:19:25.880 --> 00:19:31.050
where it's atomically
synchronizing all the writes,

00:19:31.050 --> 00:19:32.340
this works for me.

00:19:32.340 --> 00:19:34.230
So I am correct.

00:19:34.230 --> 00:19:35.540
Yes?

00:19:35.540 --> 00:19:38.840
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:38.840 --> 00:19:40.340
DANIELLE ROYSTONE:
I can't hear you.

