00:19:47.490 --> 00:19:48.121
OK, cool.

00:19:48.121 --> 00:19:48.620
Yeah.

00:19:48.620 --> 00:19:52.280
So I forgot where I
was, but it's cool.

00:19:52.280 --> 00:19:54.890
I'll figure it out.

00:19:54.890 --> 00:19:55.980
Say that again?

00:19:55.980 --> 00:19:57.230
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:57.230 --> 00:19:58.490
DANIELLE ROYSTONE:
Yes, thank you, Deepti.

00:19:58.490 --> 00:20:01.010
I was like where'd she go,
did she leave me, right.

00:20:01.010 --> 00:20:03.110
So the true time
piece, and Deepti

00:20:03.110 --> 00:20:05.462
will be able to explain
this much better than me,

00:20:05.462 --> 00:20:06.920
but the whole idea
behind true time

00:20:06.920 --> 00:20:11.090
is the ability to write
asynchronously and keep

00:20:11.090 --> 00:20:13.190
everything in sync atomically.

00:20:13.190 --> 00:20:16.400
So I no longer have a chokepoint
like I do with Oracle,

00:20:16.400 --> 00:20:19.830
and it's all being handled
for me dynamically,

00:20:19.830 --> 00:20:21.950
which is amazing.

00:20:21.950 --> 00:20:24.140
And we think that the
last piece, the true time,

00:20:24.140 --> 00:20:27.050
is really where there's
technical differentiation here

00:20:27.050 --> 00:20:29.030
versus Oracle.

00:20:29.030 --> 00:20:30.890
This is not just
any other database.

00:20:30.890 --> 00:20:33.462
This isn't marketing
fluff that we fell for.

00:20:33.462 --> 00:20:34.920
You know, oh, we
like Google, let's

00:20:34.920 --> 00:20:36.237
just pick Google's database.

00:20:36.237 --> 00:20:38.070
We looked at a bunch
of different databases.

00:20:38.070 --> 00:20:41.030
And we're like, these are
three distinct reasons

00:20:41.030 --> 00:20:44.300
that it's not only as good
as Oracle, which currently

00:20:44.300 --> 00:20:46.820
is regarded as maybe one
of the best databases,

00:20:46.820 --> 00:20:49.070
it's actually better,
right, and now

00:20:49.070 --> 00:20:52.400
it supports the dynamic
scaling that my telcos need,

00:20:52.400 --> 00:20:55.070
so they're not provisioning
data center after data center

00:20:55.070 --> 00:20:56.680
at the same cost.

00:20:56.680 --> 00:20:59.660
I'm saving them money, and
now my promise of 10 times

00:20:59.660 --> 00:21:03.150
faster at a tenth
of the cost is true.

00:21:03.150 --> 00:21:04.970
So again, I'm in India.

00:21:04.970 --> 00:21:06.590
My team's like,
don't tell the story

00:21:06.590 --> 00:21:08.639
about Oracle and public cloud.

00:21:08.639 --> 00:21:11.180
And I'm like, I'm going to do
it, I'm going to talk about it,

00:21:11.180 --> 00:21:13.460
this is like an
amazing idea, and I'm

00:21:13.460 --> 00:21:15.140
talking to the
technical leaders,

00:21:15.140 --> 00:21:18.200
and their heads just spin when
I tell them 10 times faster

00:21:18.200 --> 00:21:19.300
at a tenth of the cost.

00:21:19.300 --> 00:21:21.420
They're like, what did you say.

00:21:21.420 --> 00:21:23.990
And I was like,
yeah, I don't think

00:21:23.990 --> 00:21:26.390
you'll be interested in
it because you're so big,

00:21:26.390 --> 00:21:27.680
you know, you have
your own data centers.

00:21:27.680 --> 00:21:29.680
My team tells me you'll
never pick public cloud.

00:21:29.680 --> 00:21:32.720
They're like, can we start
a proof of concept tomorrow.

00:21:32.720 --> 00:21:34.970
And so off we go.

00:21:34.970 --> 00:21:37.040
And so that's when I
knew we were really

00:21:37.040 --> 00:21:41.750
onto a really competitive
differentiation for us

00:21:41.750 --> 00:21:43.970
as a company, and we were
going to really change

00:21:43.970 --> 00:21:47.000
the landscape for telco.

00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:48.330
All right.

00:21:48.330 --> 00:21:49.680
Migrating your application.

00:21:52.260 --> 00:21:54.450
Like I said, I think
I said this earlier,

00:21:54.450 --> 00:21:55.780
for us it was pretty easy.

00:21:55.780 --> 00:21:58.980
We had a database layer
in our application.

00:21:58.980 --> 00:22:03.420
We were able to just switch
out from basically SQL to RPC,

00:22:03.420 --> 00:22:06.220
and then we started
performance benchmarking it,

00:22:06.220 --> 00:22:07.620
and it was 10 times faster.

00:22:07.620 --> 00:22:08.920
I mean, that sounds easy.

00:22:08.920 --> 00:22:11.400
I'm sure if the engineers
that worked on it were here,

00:22:11.400 --> 00:22:14.540
they'd be like, uh, a little
bit more went into that work.

00:22:14.540 --> 00:22:19.410
But I'd say within 90
days, we had something

00:22:19.410 --> 00:22:22.080
that was 10 times faster,
and then within six months,

00:22:22.080 --> 00:22:24.660
we had something that was
10 times cheaper, right.

00:22:24.660 --> 00:22:27.120
So at first, we were deploying
like a ridiculous number

00:22:27.120 --> 00:22:32.070
of nodes, and then later we got
a little bit more efficient,

00:22:32.070 --> 00:22:33.630
so that was cool.

00:22:33.630 --> 00:22:37.920
We think there's still room to
make this even faster, right.

00:22:37.920 --> 00:22:41.084
So 10 times, I mean, like
I said, turning heads.

00:22:41.084 --> 00:22:43.500
I have a lot of customers that
are in the audience, right.

00:22:43.500 --> 00:22:45.619
We spent time yesterday
talking about it.

00:22:45.619 --> 00:22:47.410
You know, they're
excited about it as well.

00:22:47.410 --> 00:22:51.600
So it's working for small
telcos, for super big telcos.

00:22:51.600 --> 00:22:53.670
It's a really great idea.

00:22:53.670 --> 00:22:57.330
We think there's still two
more features of Spanner

00:22:57.330 --> 00:23:00.870
that we could do to take it
kind of to the next level

00:23:00.870 --> 00:23:02.960
if we ever needed to.

00:23:02.960 --> 00:23:06.450
They have an interesting
parent child relationship

00:23:06.450 --> 00:23:07.950
with the data, so
you can kind of

00:23:07.950 --> 00:23:11.550
bring your data closer together,
so that when we do a look up

00:23:11.550 --> 00:23:14.940
on a particular customer's
plan, it's a little bit faster.

00:23:14.940 --> 00:23:17.400
And so we're not doing
that yet, but we could.

00:23:17.400 --> 00:23:20.010
And then we could
look at the way

00:23:20.010 --> 00:23:22.560
we're designing our
database transaction

00:23:22.560 --> 00:23:25.440
boundaries so that
it's a little bit more

00:23:25.440 --> 00:23:28.470
efficient and optimized.

00:23:28.470 --> 00:23:30.210
We haven't done that.

00:23:30.210 --> 00:23:33.840
Right now, we're really
focused on getting this older--

00:23:33.840 --> 00:23:36.710
I mean, this application
is 15 years old, right.

00:23:36.710 --> 00:23:40.650
This isn't like
Instagram, Snapchat.

00:23:40.650 --> 00:23:43.680
It's like old and boring, like
I said at the beginning, right,

00:23:43.680 --> 00:23:48.630
but it's running billions
of dollars of monetization

00:23:48.630 --> 00:23:50.160
for the telcos of the world.

00:23:50.160 --> 00:23:52.050
Every telco has a
product like this.

00:23:52.050 --> 00:23:54.810
Some cases, they have multiple
products running, right.

00:23:54.810 --> 00:24:00.210
So we think this is incredible
technology, game changing

00:24:00.210 --> 00:24:05.820
computer science stuff, and so
we're super excited about that.

